## Extending the Benchmark

The construction workflow converts a public, executable coding task into an
AsynCodeBench instance by identifying natural subproblems, labeling observable
producer/consumer dependencies, defining the four controlled protocols,
creating dependency probes, and running automatic validation gates.

The reusable construction instructions and validation gates are documented in:

```text
docs/SECTION_2_BENCHMARK_CONSTRUCTION_MATERIALS.md
docs/protocols/COMMIT0_DATA_AND_METRIC_LABEL_GUIDE_v0.3.md
```

The construction scripts and generated manifests make benchmark construction
auditable; they do not replace contract tests or the required human review.
Empty required forms remain explicit pending work. Optional secondary-review
and adjudication templates are retained for additional auditing and provenance.

## Bring Your Own Agent

The default `openhands` adapter preserves the validated experiments. A
third-party agent can be loaded without editing the benchmark:

```bash
cd reproductions/async-swe-agents
uv run asyncodebench run \
  --task asyncodebench:cachetools \
  --protocol all \
  --model "$LLM_MODEL" \
  --agent-import-path my_agents.cache_agent:CacheAgent \
  --run-id custom-agent-v01
```

The adapter receives only the active assignment, workspace handle, writable
paths, targeted tests, dependency labels, and budget. The harness independently
checks its artifact and computes the official result. See
[`docs/AGENT_ADAPTER.md`](docs/AGENT_ADAPTER.md).

